<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Romania on Typophile Archive</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/romania/</link><description>Recent content in Romania on Typophile Archive</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 29 Jul 2013 11:08:00 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/romania/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Typography under Ceaușescu</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/105032/</link><pubDate>Mon, 29 Jul 2013 11:08:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/105032/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;cite&gt;&lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20141017103151/http:/www.the-autobiography.com/" title="Official site" rel="nofollow"&gt;The Autobiography of Nicolae Ceaușescu&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/cite&gt; is a 2010 documentary by Andrei Ujică. It’s about Nicolae Ceaușescu but uses nothing but archival footage to tell its story.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And, every couple of minutes, there’s one amazing piece of public typography (signage, banners, parade floats) after another. I put together &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20141017103151/http:/www.flickr.com/photos/joeclark/sets/72157634849676232/?detail=1" title="Typography in ‘The Autobiography of Nicolae Ceaușescu’"&gt;a Flickr set of screenshots&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
